/* Columbia County Live — service worker (X20 Round 4: "It works out there")
// v93 = Kneeland Brothers LLC takes Founding Sponsor Spot 2 (Matt Kneeland said yes Nov 16): Spot 2 panel cell with logo, FS medallion and Malarkey Certified Contractor line, tap-to-open full card (pv-sheet), Local Services directory listing right after Oregon Trail Lanes (no map pin, no verified coordinates), Community Partners wall entry; counts read 2 filled / 1 open; CSS gains the ribbon placement block (directory ribbons over the photo top-left, never crowding a phone number); SW ccl-v93, cache-bust ?v=93
 *
 * Makes the page an offline-capable, instant-return app for a river corridor
 * with real dead zones. Strategy by request kind — live data is NEVER cached:
 *
 *  - Navigations, shell code (CSS/JS), the county GeoJSON, the manifest:
 *    network-first, cache fallback — freshest code wins when there's signal,
 *    the last good copy carries you when there isn't.
 *  - data/*.json snapshots: network-first, cache fallback. What you see
 *    offline is the last real collection, still stamped with its true
 *    collection time; the freshness board shows its real, growing age.
 *    The snapshots are carried forward across version bumps, so an
 *    offline launch right after an update still has them.
 *  - Images and icons: the shell's own art is precached with the shell
 *    and served cache-first; every other image is stale-while-revalidate
 *    — a photo or logo swapped at the same URL shows its last copy once,
 *    then the new one, and can never stay stuck on the old file all
 *    version long.
 *  - Versioned library CDNs (Leaflet, Google Fonts): cache-first, refreshed
 *    in the background.
 *  - Everything else cross-origin (NWS, USGS, ADS-B, the ISS feed, map
 *    tiles, radar): NOT intercepted. Offline, those feeds simply fail and
 *    their panels say so — nothing is faked, nothing pretends to be live.
 */
